Q: XMLHttpRequest to php and fake parameters A: <p>There are a few things you can do:</p>
<p>First of all, and this is absolutely necessary, use <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/mysqli.quickstart.prepared-statements.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">prepared statements</a> for all PHP variables you put in a database query. This will prevent <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/security.database.sql-injection.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SQL injection</a>, the most common way to attack a web site.</p>
<p>Secondly, assume the user will put in garbage. That means you need to validate anything that comes in. In case of an email, you can use:</p>
<pre><code>filter_input(INPUT_POST, &quot;receive_product_updates_email&quot;, FILTER_VALIDATE_EMAIL)
</code></pre>
<p>to validate the email address. Only accept it when it is valid. The user ID is probably an integer, so you can use:</p>
<pre><code>filter_input(INPUT_POST, &quot;user_id&quot;, FILTER_VALIDATE_INT)
</code></pre>
<p>However, if this is just the currently active user it's better to get it from the user session, that way a hacker cannot easily imitate multiple users.</p>
<p>Thirdly, since there are probably multiple user IDs that can be send in, you need to validate the user sending you this information. There are several ways. You can check whether the user is logged in, assuming that logging in is needed before submitting a new email address. If logging in is not required, and I think it should, you could send a random token along with the form data which also needs to be validated. You can even do this when logging in is required. See for an example implementation: <a href="https://stevenroland.com/posts/implementing-single-use-tokens-with-php-sessions/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Implementing Single-Use Tokens with PHP Sessions</a>. Many variations on this topic exist. Just always make sure that the user sending in the information is authorized to do so.</p>
<p>These three basic steps will tackle the main problems with your code.</p>
<p>There's also a modern JavaScript replacement for <code>XMLHttpRequest</code> called <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>fetch()</code></a>. In your case you could replace <code>XMLHttpRequest</code> with something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const params = new FormData();

params.append('update_receive_product_updates', 1);
params.append('receive_product_updates_email', receive_product_updates_email);
params.append('user_id', userLicencesTable.getAttribute('data-user-id'));

fetch('product_management.php', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: params
})
  .then(response =&gt; {
    // handle the fetch response here.
  })
  .catch(error =&gt; {
    // if there's an error, handle it here
  })
</code></pre>

Q: Duplicate call of FilterChainProxy with SpringSecurity, MVC and StringResponseBody A: <p>Currently, I'm trying to dive deep into spring security, and i'm not sure about ur case.<br />
but you should see this point mentioned in the Docs <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/architecture.html#_declaring_your_filter_as_a_bean" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/architecture.html#_declaring_your_filter_as_a_bean</a>.</p>
<p>You mention the filter is &quot;configured as a <strong>Bean</strong> in a <code>@Configuration</code> object&quot;. If <code>AuthenticationFilter</code> is exposed as a bean of type <code>Filter</code>, Spring Boot will also auto-register it in the plain servlet filter chain, outside <code>FilterChainProxy</code>.</p>
<p>if you need it as a bean, disable the automatic registration:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>@Bean
public FilterRegistrationBean&lt;AuthenticationFilter&gt;tenantFilterRegistration(TenantFilter filter) {
    FilterRegistrationBean&lt;AuthenticationFilter&gt; registration = new FilterRegistrationBean&lt;&gt;(filter);
    registration.setEnabled(false);
    return registration;
}
</code></pre>
